---
import Icon from '~/components/common/Icon.astro';

import DshArticleLayout from '~/layouts/DshArticleLayout.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'DSH Mobile Remote security model',
  description: 'Documented trust boundaries, stored data, transport protection, and current MVP limitations.',
  url: 'https://dshmobile.online/security/',
  codeRepository: 'https://github.com/april-jk/dsh-mobile-suite',
};
---

<DshArticleLayout
  title="DSH Mobile Remote security model"
  description="Understand the outbound connection, device credentials, short-lived WebView tickets, Relay data, and current MVP limitations before pairing a phone."
  icon="tabler:shield-lock"
  structuredData={schema}
>
  <div
    class="not-prose rounded-xl border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 p-6 text-amber-950 dark:border-amber-700 dark:bg-amber-950/40 dark:text-amber-100"
  >
    <div class="flex gap-4">
      <Icon name="tabler:alert-triangle" class="mt-0.5 size-6 shrink-0" />
      <p class="leading-7">
        The current MVP uses HTTPS/WSS but does not provide application-level end-to-end encryption. The Relay process
        can see content while forwarding it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Network boundary</h2>
  <p>
    DeepSeek Harness remains bound to <code>127.0.0.1:3080</code>. The Companion does not create a public listener. It
    opens an outbound authenticated WSS connection to the configured Relay and rewrites the upstream Host header back to
    the local DSH listener.
  </p>

  <h2>Credentials</h2>
  <div class="info-grid not-prose">
    <section>
      <Icon name="tabler:device-mobile" class="size-8 text-primary" />
      <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-bold">Mobile client</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">
        Uses account access tokens and short-lived WebView tickets. It never receives the computer device token.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <Icon name="tabler:device-laptop" class="size-8 text-primary" />
      <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-bold">Computer</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">
        Stores the Relay device token locally in <code>~/.dsh-remote/config.json</code> with owner-only permissions.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <Icon name="tabler:database" class="size-8 text-primary" />
      <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-bold">Relay</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">
        Stores hashes of passwords, refresh tokens, and device tokens rather than the original values.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <Icon name="tabler:clock-shield" class="size-8 text-primary" />
      <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-bold">Remote session</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">
        Uses a short-lived ticket tied to the authenticated mobile account and selected device.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>What the Relay stores</h2>
  <p>
    The Relay stores accounts, devices, pairing state, and bounded access-log metadata such as phone information and
    access times. It does not persist forwarded DSH HTTP or WebSocket request and response bodies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not persisting payloads is not the same as end-to-end encryption. The Relay process can still observe content while
    forwarding it in the current release.
  </p>

  <h2>Revocation</h2>
  <p>
    Removing a pairing revokes the Relay device credential, disconnects active remote access, and clears the local
    credential after the Relay confirms the operation. The <code>dsh-mobile unpair</code> command provides the same operation
    when the Web UI is unavailable.
  </p>

  <h2>Known MVP limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No application-level end-to-end encryption.</li>
    <li>No email verification or account recovery.</li>
    <li>The Relay must run as a single instance.</li>
    <li>
      Account deletion, a full privacy policy, and related compliance work are required before app-store distribution.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Operate a tighter boundary</h2>
  <p>
    You can <a href="/self-hosted-relay/">deploy your own Relay</a> and control its host, TLS termination, storage, and logs.
    The same MVP encryption limitation still applies.
  </p>

  <p>
    Report security issues privately through the process in the Suite
    <a href="https://github.com/april-jk/dsh-mobile-suite/blob/main/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md</a>. Do not publish
    exploitable details in a public issue.
  </p>
</DshArticleLayout>
